arch 2013.\n- ^ Felix A, Pitt RM (July 1934). "A New Antigen of B. Typhosus". The Lancet. 224 (5787): 186–191. doi:10.1016/S0140-6736(00)44360-6.\n- ^ Craigie J (1 November 1957). "Arthur Felix, 1887–1956". Biographical Memoirs of Fellows of the Royal Society. 3 (3): 53–79. doi:10.1098/rsbm.1957.0005. S2CID 72753150.\n- ^ "Typhoid Fever". Center for Emerging and Re-emerging Infectious Diseases. University of Washington. Archived from the original on 8 December 2019. Retrieved 8 December 2019.\n- ^ "TYPHOID OR DRAIN FEVER, AND HOW TO PREVENT ITS SPREADING". The Warwick Examiner and Times. 6 April 1878. Retrieved 13 April 2020.\n- ^ Platner SB, Ashby T (2002). A Topographical Dictionary of Ancient Rome. Oxford University Press. ISBN 978-0-19-925649-5.\n- ^ Yapijakis C (2009). "Hippocrates of Kos, the father of clinical medicine, and Asclepiades of Bithynia, the father of molecular medicine. Review". In Vivo (Athens, Greece). 23 (4): 507–514. PMID\xa019567383. The Greek physician Antonius Musa notably cured the emperor Augustus of typhoid fever with cold baths\n- ^ Darby E (1983). The cult of the Prince Consort. Nicola Smith. New Haven: Yale University Press. ISBN 0-300-03015-0. OCLC 10432279.\n- ^ Matthew HC (September 2004). "Edward VII (1841–1910)". Oxford Dictionary of National Biography (online ed.). Oxford University Press (published May 2006). doi:10.1093/ref:odnb/32975. Archived from the original on 2 March 2016. (Subscription, Wikipedia Library access or UK public library membership required.)\n- ^ "Nicholas II - At the Court of the Last Tsar - Chapter 1, Part 2, The Empress Alexandra". www.alexanderpalace.org. Retrieved 21 June 2021.\n- ^ "Hoogleraar heeft aanwijzingen voor abortus bij Wilhelmina: \'Monarchie hing aan zijden draadje\'" [Professor has indication for abortion Wilhelmina: Monarchy was hanging by a thread]. RTL Nieuws (in Dutch). 19 April 2023.\n- ^ Trudy Dehue. "Egg Foetus Baby, A New History of Pregnancy". Dutch foundation for literature. Archived from the original on 17 March 2024. Retrieved 6 December 2023.\n- ^ Dennis B (29 September 2011). "Willie Lincoln\'s death: A private agony for a president facing a nation of pain". The Washington Post. ISSN 0190-8286. Archived from the original on 1 April 2017. Retrieved 12 March 2017.\n- ^ "Maldiçãp, febre e alucinações: a melancólica morte de Leopoldina de Bragança". aventurasnahistoria.uol.com.br. 7 June 2020. Retrieved 3 February 2022.\n- ^ "A History of Stanford". Stanford University. Retrieved 4 July 2018.\n- ^ Ruggles E (1944). Gerard Manley Hopkins: A Life. Norton.\n- ^ Scott BF (1976). "Henry James Herbert Scott (1858–1910)". Australian Dictionary of Biography. Vol. 6. National Centre of Biography, Australian National University. ISBN 978-0-522-84459-7. ISSN 1833-7538. OCLC 70677943. Retrieved 30 August 2025.\n- ^ "Straw for Silence". The Spectator. Vol. 203. F.C. Westley. 1959. ISSN 0038-6952. OCLC 1766325.\nFurther reading\n[edit]\n- "Typhoid and Paratyphoid Fever: Information for Health Professionals". Centers for Disease Control and Prevention. 30 March 2021. Retrieved 27 June 2021.\n- Stanaway JD, et al. (GBD 2017 Typhoid and Paratyphoid Collaborators) (April 2019). "The global burden of typhoid and paratyphoid fevers: a systematic analysis for the Global Burden of Disease Study 2017". The Lancet Infectious Diseases. 19 (4): 369–381. Bibcode:2019LanID..19..369S. doi:10.1016/S1473-3099(18)30685-6. PMC 6437314. PMID 30792131.\n- "Typhoid Fever: Overview". National Health Service. 18 June 2018. Retrieved 27 June 2021.\n- Public Health Agency of Canada (2010). "Pathogen Safety Data Sheets: Infectious Substances – Salmonella enterica spp". Retrieved 28 June 2021.\n- "Typhoid: Fact Sheet". 31 January 2018. Retrieved 27 June 2021.', 0.6573698316113556)]
answer=The physician was **John Snow**. The documents say he “identified the outbreak's source as the public water pump on Broad Street” and later “used a dot map to illustrate how cases of cholera occurred around this pump.”

He visually proved the connection by mapping the deaths near the pump: “**Snow later used a dot map to illustrate how cases of cholera occurred around this pump**,” and his map showed that “**the section of Snow's map representing areas in the city where the closest available source of water was the Broad Street pump had the highest incidence of cholera**.”
